window.docContents[135].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow":{"contents":"\n\nRemoving viscosity from the momentum law leaves the incompressible Euler\nequations:\n\n\n\nThe equation describes an ideal fluid with no viscous dissipation. Its\nnonlinearity is the vector analogue of Burgers self-transport, while pressure\ncouples all components through incompressibility.\n\nThe Euler momentum residual is the vector fieldThe pointwise momentum balance is the equation .\n\nThe residual vanishes exactly when\n\nMove the force term across the equality. This is an identity in the additive\ngroup of velocity vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow","header":"8.4.1. Incompressible Euler Flow","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--Verified-computation-and-the-open-problem":{"contents":"For the exact rotation and forced shear flows, the symbolic proofs establish\nincompressibility and vanishing of each momentum residual component in Lean.\nNeither velocity has finite energy,\nso neither is admissible data for the Clay alternatives. On rational spacetime\nboxes, CEGA can certify scalar bounds for supported residual expressions after\ntheir derivative formulas have been justified. A bound in a chosen vector or\nfunction norm needs an explicit reduction to these certificates; with that\nreduction, estimates take the form\n\n\n\nA computer-assisted proof for a nontrivial Navier–Stokes regime would combine\nsuch bounds with an analytic validation theorem converting residual bounds into\na nearby true solution, quantitative control of a continuation norm, and an\nargument covering the entire time interval. The exact CAS checks, the interval\ncertificates, and the abstract continuation alternative are formalized; such a\nvalidation theorem is not.\n\nThe simplest solved datum checks that the formal Clay statement connects\ncorrectly to the core equation.\n\nFor every positive viscosity, the zero initial velocity with zero forcing has\na global smooth finite-energy solution:Its kinetic energy vanishes for every nonnegative time, so the existence\npredicate of alternative (A) holds for this datum.\n\nThe global zero classical solution supplies smooth velocity and pressure on\nthe physical half-space. The initial velocity is divergence free, and it and\nthe force are smooth and rapidly decreasing because they vanish identically.\nEvery term in the momentum equation is zero, and the kinetic energy is zero at\nevery time.\nAny positive constant therefore gives the strict uniform energy bound required\nby alternative (A) for this datum.\n\nAlternative (A) asks for the same conclusion for every admissible initial\nvelocity; alternative (C) asks, at each viscosity, for one admissible datum and\nforce where it fails.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009The Three-Dimensional Regularity Problem\u0009The Navier–Stokes Millennium Problem","header":"8.7.1.5. Verified computation and the open problem","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--Verified-computation-and-the-open-problem"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--One-dimensional-and-coordinate-line-calculus":{"contents":"Every global  class has a selected representative that:\n\n* is absolutely continuous on every compact interval;* has classical derivative equal almost everywhere to the stored weak\n  derivative;* satisfies the literal fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nAbsolute value, positive and negative parts, and interval truncation have the\nexpected almost-everywhere derivative formulas for this representative.  They\nare also completed operations on , so later\nproofs need not rebuild graph-space elements from raw representatives.\n\nIn higher dimensions, coordinate insertion and Fubini give absolutely\ncontinuous representatives on almost every coordinate line.  The classical\nline derivative agrees almost everywhere with the corresponding weak partial\nderivative.  A two-way coordinate ACL characterization is available in the\nfinite-exponent setting used by the nonsmooth chain rule.\n\nThe line theorem is coordinatewise: absolute continuity holds on almost every\nline parallel to a coordinate axis.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.9. One-dimensional and coordinate-line calculus","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--One-dimensional-and-coordinate-line-calculus"}});